<!--
    A function that calls itself with its own incoming register untouched keeps
    that argument at the call.

        unsigned mix(unsigned char *p, unsigned n, unsigned key) {
          p[n & 3] ^= (unsigned char)(key + 25);
          if (n == 0) return p[0] & 1;
          return !mix(p, (n - 1) & 7, key);
        }

    gcc -O2 leaves `key` in edx for the recursive call, and no op of the call's
    setup names edx, so the call gets no argument for it (heritage registers a
    trial only for a range some op reads or writes, and upstream's
    AncestorRealistic refuses the function's own input at a call slot). With the
    option off kuna prints `mix(a0,a1 - 1 & 7)`, and `key` is only the `char` the
    store reads.

    Stage / sub-stage: P4 "active-input-trial-scoring"
    (`p4_calls/kuna_passthrough.rs` `stated_width` / `stated_registers`, with
    `p4_calls/kuna_entryreads.rs` taking the body's reads at the first heritage).
    No callee was decompiled first, so nothing is stated: the only evidence is
    the function's own body, which reads edx (`lea 0x19(%rdx),%ecx`), so the
    recursive call takes it and the parameter is the four bytes that read's
    result needs, not the byte the store keeps.

    P0 assertion: `option passthrough on|off` (on by default). Pass 1 is the bug
    (off), pass 2 the fix (on). The decompile-all witnesses (a callee that reads
    its parameter only by handing it to fcntl, through a forwarder and through a
    value the caller also compares) are
    decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/passthrough_bodyreads_x86_64
    under tests/cli/passthrough-keeps-an-argument-the-callee-body-takes.json.
-->
